# Store hierarchy: one ABC, shared surface through every layer

Carl's call (2026-08-23): Store/LocalStore/RemoteStore share
practically all API surface - Store IS the ABC, mirroring upstream
Nix. Today that shape survives C++ -> pxd (CLocalStore(CStore)) but
dissolves above it: emitted Async* classes are flat and standalone,
and the gRPC layer emits LocalStoreService/RemoteStoreService as
unrelated duplicates of the same method list.

## Goal

Model the base-class relationship once and let every layer derive
from it: sync bindings, generated async wrappers, manifest, gRPC
schema. Concrete subclasses add or restrict; nothing duplicates.

## Design questions

- Emitted AsyncStore as a real base carrying the hop-method bodies
  (they are identical: self._runner.call(...)); subclasses contribute
  __init__/runner and their POLICY-FILTERED surface. Tension to
  resolve: LocalStore is pool, RemoteStore is affine, and policy
  drops diverge (query_derivation exists only on RemoteStore).
  Inheritance plus per-subclass hiding needs a clean mechanism -
  candidates: base holds everything and subclasses shadow-drops, or
  emitter flattens per subclass but VALIDATES against the shared set.
- gRPC: proto has no inheritance. Keep per-concrete services but
  generate their method sets from the deduplicated Store surface;
  consider whether reflection clients should see a StoreService.
- Manifest gains explicit bases/borrowed-from metadata so the
  protocol layer (017) and external tools see the true shape.
- Upstream alignment: real Nix has an abstract refcounted Store base;
  getting this right now makes the 015 spike a substitution, not a
  rewrite.

Depends on nothing open; synergizes with 017 and 015.

## Done 2026-08-25

Carl's call, restated: "Store should definitely exist, it's what will
be used the most by real bindings since you don't want to care about
what store type you're resolving to." That decides the design question
the task left open, because it makes the base a THING CALLERS HOLD,
not an implementation detail of the emitter.

Store stops being `_async = False` and becomes `_abstract = True`:
generated, wire-addressable, never constructed. A bare Store() is a
trampoline whose Python overrides do not exist, so refusing to build
one loses nothing.

## The tension, resolved

The base carries the INTERSECTION of what its subclasses actually
expose AFTER their own policy filtering. That is exactly the set a
caller can use without knowing which implementation it holds - which
is what an abstract Store is for - so the rule and the purpose are the
same sentence.

Neither candidate from the task was needed. Nothing shadow-drops: a
method the base does not promise simply is not on it. And validation
is not an alternative to inheritance but a companion to it - the
generator checks that every shared method has the SAME signature on
base and subclass, because a subclass whose types drifted would
inherit the base's body and lie about them.

    Store         get_uri, is_valid_path, add_text_to_store,
                  build_derivation          (guaranteed)
    LocalStore    - nothing but a constructor
    RemoteStore   query_derivation          (pool policy drops it from
                                             LocalStore, so it is not
                                             guaranteed)

## gRPC

Proto has no inheritance, so each method is declared exactly ONCE, on
the service that owns it. Shared store methods live on StoreService;
a LocalStore handle calls them there. RemoteObj walks the base chain
the way Python does and lands on the declaring service.

That is the type-agnostic path working for real: one service, either
implementation, and the server resolves the handle to whatever wrapper
it holds. An external grpcurl call does the same.

The alternative - concrete services repeating their inherited surface
- would let a client use one service per class, at the cost of
duplicate req/resp messages for every inherited method on every
subclass. Rejected: it is schema bloat in exchange for not knowing
about a base the reflection already describes.

## It unblocked 021 in passing

describe(obj: Store) had NO rpc at all, because Store was excluded
from generation and so had no wire policy. It crosses now, for either
implementation. The build stopped printing that blocker on its own -
the surest sign the two tasks were the same problem.

## Left open

017 (protocols) is the natural follow-on and now has a real base to
mirror. Note that AsyncStore is already close to the Protocol that
task wants for the in-process side; the open question there is
unchanged and is about the REMOTE side's return types.
